# Line endings
#
# Normalise every text file to LF in the repository, and check it out as LF on
# every platform. The explicit `eol=lf` is the part that matters: with only
# `text=auto`, Git stores LF but hands Windows a CRLF working tree, so the tree
# never matches the index and `git add` warns on every file.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that genuinely require CRLF. Batch scripts are the real case - cmd.exe
# can mis-parse labels and `goto` targets in an LF file.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Files that break if they ever acquire CRLF, stated explicitly so an override
# above can never reach them. A shell script with CRLF fails on its shebang;
# a Makefile fails on its recipe lines.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf
*.mk        text eol=lf

# Documentation
#
# Git's markdown diff driver puts the enclosing heading in the hunk header,
# which is worth having in a repository that is almost entirely prose.
*.md    text eol=lf diff=markdown

# Never normalise, never diff as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.gif   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip   binary
